abstract:
Extreme Gradient Boosting. The same residual loop as Topic 67, but with a second-order Taylor expansion of the loss, a regularisation term baked into the objective, and an engineering stack — histogram binning, sparsity awareness, cache-aware column blocks, parallel split finding — that makes it the default choice for tabular competitions. We derive the similarity score and gain formulas, then measure every regularisation knob.}
\usepackage{mlstudy}
\begin{document}
\MLtitlepage
\tableofcontents
\newpage

\section{From the notes

blocks.t:
\text{Similarity}=\frac{(\sum \text{residuals})^2}{\text{number of residuals}+\lambda}
\qquad
\text{Gain}=\text{Sim}_{\text{left}}+\text{Sim}_{\text{right}}-\text{Sim}_{\text{root}}

\boxed{ w_j^{\star} = -\frac{G_j}{H_j+\lambda} }
\qquad
\mathcal{L}^{\star} = -\frac12 \sum_{j=1}^{T}\frac{G_j^2}{H_j+\lambda} + \gamma T .

The notes' five improvements, mapped:
regularisation $\to$ $\lambda,\alpha$ inside the objective;
missing values $\to$ learned default direction per split;
parallel processing $\to$ parallel split search within a tree (not across trees);
pruning $\to$ $\gamma$, applied to gain;
cross validation $\to$ xgb.cv with early_stopping_rounds.
